Files here: the ten parts' STEP models (N.step) and G-code programs (N.ngc), unchanged, as
downloaded 2026-11-24 (each checked against the record's MD5; SHA-256 in SHA256SUMS). The
record's rendered images of the machined parts are not copied.

From the record's description (not ours): all parts are made from a raw block of 80 x 80 x 50 mm
with one end mill of at most 6 mm diameter, single setup, on a three-axis machine; the programs
were made with Autodesk CAM (their header: "HERSTELLER AUTODESK", "MODEL GENERIC 3-AXIS").

What we read off the programs (verify/forge-cam-quality, --ngc): every program faces the block at
Z -1 (X from about -3.6 to 84.9, Y from about -0.5 to 78, the tool 6 mm) with one 6 mm flat end mill (T1 D=6. CR=0.), so the block is taken as
X 0..80, Y 0..80, Z -50..0 in the program's frame (G54). That is our reading, not the authors'
statement: their description gives the block's size, not where G54 is on it.

Used here as an outside reference: the same parts, another CAM system's toolpaths, measured with
the same model as forge-cam's own (verify/forge-cam-quality/README.md, "Foreign programs").
